it on the item \u2014 renew the `revisit:` date, or close it out'. The new owner line adds a third outcome, doing the work the item scheduled, and that is not a backlog metadata edit. A maintainer reading the comment to judge whether `/prawduct:backlog` is still the right pointer is told something that is now only two-thirds true. Either make the clause relational (the item's own record changes in every case: a renewed date, a closure, or a closure once the scheduled work ships) or delete it. This is a comment, and no gate reads it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/norm_probes.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "New shipped comment and test docstring narrate the old wording's history", "recommendation": "Instance. The added comment ('Naming only the first told the owner to renew or lapse an item whose date had simply arrived, so both are named here') and the test docstring ('The owner line once named only the first') describe what the code used to say. Per the Severity Levels prose remedies, a comment that narrates history is a deletion. The comment's first sentence, which names the two intents `revisit:` carries, is enough to stand alone. The test also asserts `not ...startswith(\"Exception clock expired\")`, which pins the absence of a retired string. That is harmless, but it is history too.", "files": ["plugin/lib/norm_probes.py", "tests/test_norm_probes.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The second meaning of `revisit:` reached the skill and the probe, but not the other surfaces that describe the field", "recommendation": "Class. The cause is that the field's meaning was widened at the two sites that motivated it. Core rule 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees' applies. `plugin/templates/backlog.md` line 60 (the scaffolded legend, which the backlog skill's legend refresh reconciles against), `plugin/lib/backlog/legacy.py` `revisit` docstring, and the probe's own evidence string ('past-dated `revisit:` exception clock') still describe the field only as an exception or stopgap expiry. The evidence string stays unchanged on purpose, for id stability, and the change fragment says so. That reason is recorded only in the fragment, not at the site, so a later wording fix there would re-mint the advisory id. None of these descriptions is false: an exception is still one use. A product scaffolded from the template simply never learns the second use. To bound it, grep `revisit` across plugin/templates, plugin/lib/backlog and plugin/docs/norms.md \u00a7 Exceptions expire.", "files": ["plugin/lib/norm_probes.py", "plugin/skills/backlog/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-11"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `develop`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "base_reviewed": null, "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/changes/delegate-scratch-and-revisit-wording.md", "plugin/methodology/delegation.md", "plugin/skills/backlog/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0}}, "span": null, "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": null, "dispatched_at": "2026-11-22T22:34:17Z", "tree_changed_during_review": {"paths": [".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.9.0.md", ".prawduct/changes/delegate-scratch-and-revisit-wording.md"], "reason": "builder added the fragment's missing Consumer note section (prose, no claim change) and drafted the release plan (a release artifact outside the reviewed fix) while the review ran"}}}
